WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.300
A sugar is added to a protein inside the ER of a cultured mammalian cell.

2
00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.320
Later, that sugar sits on the outside of the cell.

3
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:17.380
Yet the protein never crossed a membrane on the way.

4
00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:22.740
So how does a sugar made inside the ER end up on the cell surface?

5
00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:27.920
The answer is one rule about the two faces of every membrane.

6
00:00:28.783 --> 00:00:34.363
A membrane is two sheets of lipid, and each sheet is called a leaflet.

7
00:00:34.903 --> 00:00:37.343
So every membrane has two faces.

8
00:00:38.303 --> 00:00:45.203
In the plasma membrane, ER, Golgi and endosomes, one face looks at the cytosol, the

9
00:00:45.203 --> 00:00:48.123
fluid that fills the cell outside the organelles.

10
00:00:48.783 --> 00:00:54.063
The other faces the outside of the cell, or the lumen inside an organelle such as

11
00:00:54.063 --> 00:01:00.663
the ER. Lipids and proteins slide sideways within their own leaflet, but cannot

12
00:01:00.663 --> 00:01:02.103
easily flip to the other.

13
00:01:02.933 --> 00:01:06.493
Take a protein with one membrane-spanning segment.

14
00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:09.733
Its N terminus is in the lumen of the ER.

15
00:01:10.273 --> 00:01:15.553
Its C terminus is in the cytosol, which makes it a single-pass type I protein.

16
00:01:16.233 --> 00:01:19.713
The N-terminal domain carries a sugar, added in the lumen.

17
00:01:20.473 --> 00:01:25.573
This direction is set once, when the protein enters the ER membrane, usually through

18
00:01:25.573 --> 00:01:26.573
the translocon.

19
00:01:27.167 --> 00:01:33.447
Here is the rule: a lumen is topologically equivalent to the outside of the cell.

20
00:01:34.247 --> 00:01:38.307
A transport vesicle buds from one membrane and fuses with another.

21
00:01:39.267 --> 00:01:44.227
Its lumen is continuous first with the donor lumen, and then with the target lumen.

22
00:01:45.187 --> 00:01:47.207
Nothing crosses a membrane during the trip.

23
00:01:47.967 --> 00:01:51.667
The face that pointed at the cytosol keeps pointing at the cytosol.

24
00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:59.190
Follow the protein through the Golgi by vesicles, and the lumenal face stays

25
00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:03.850
lumenal. A secretory vesicle fuses with the plasma membrane.

26
00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:06.430
Its lumen opens to the outside.

27
00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:11.730
The N terminus and the sugar now face the extracellular fluid.

28
00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:14.850
The C terminus stays in the cytosol.

29
00:02:16.534 --> 00:02:21.894
Endocytosis pinches the plasma membrane inward, and the outside face becomes the

30
00:02:21.894 --> 00:02:23.254
lumen of an endosome.

31
00:02:23.934 --> 00:02:28.874
The N terminus and the sugar face that lumen, and the C terminus still faces the

32
00:02:28.874 --> 00:02:33.214
cytosol. At no step did the C terminus leave the cytosol.

33
00:02:33.874 --> 00:02:37.514
That is how a sugar made in the ER reaches the cell surface.

34
00:02:38.394 --> 00:02:44.274
Keep the rule: orientation is set once, at the ER, and traffic never flips it.
